# 1. “Difference entails substrate”

## Your argument

[
D(0,1)\rightarrow S.
]

The thought is that two distinguishable terms must share something within which they can differ.

## What is valid

A distinction requires a **contrast structure**:

[
D(0,1)
\Rightarrow
\exists\mathcal C
\text{ in which }0\neq1.
]

At minimum, there must be:

* terms;
* a relation of nonidentity;
* some logical or representational domain in which the relation is expressible.

So this is defensible:

[
\boxed{
\text{difference presupposes a space of articulation.}
}
]

## What is not valid

It does not follow that the terms are expressions of one ontological substance.

A relation can be treated as primitive:

[
D(0,1)
]

without introducing:

[
0=S(a),
\qquad
1=S(b).
]

Two objects may share:

* a logical domain;
* a category;
* a comparison relation;
* a universe of discourse;

without being modifications of one metaphysical substrate.

The word (S) therefore slides between three very different meanings:

[
\begin{aligned}
S_L&=\text{logical domain};\
S_F&=\text{formal state space};\
S_O&=\text{ontological ground or substance}.
\end{aligned}
]

Difference may entail something like (S_L).

A physical theory may posit (S_F).

It does not deductively establish (S_O).

The valid conclusion is:

[
\boxed{
D(0,1)
\Rightarrow
\text{relational or contrastive structure}.
}
]

The stronger conclusion:

[
\boxed{
D(0,1)
\Rightarrow
\text{one common metaphysical substrate}
}
]

is unsupported.

