# 2. “Pure distinction is Shannon information”

This is not correct as stated.

Shannon information concerns uncertainty over a probability distribution and the information transmitted through communication systems. For an event of probability (p), its information is commonly represented as:

[
I(x)=-\log p(x),
]

while entropy is:

[
H(X)=-\sum_x p(x)\log p(x).
]

A binary distinction can encode one bit only under specific assumptions—for example, two equiprobable alternatives. Shannon information is not “what remains when the substrate is subtracted,” nor is (i) a universal ontological atom of difference. ([Harvard Mathematics Department][1])

You may define an abstract distinction token:

[
i:=D(0,1),
]

but it should be called:

[
\text{formal distinction},
]

not automatically:

[
\text{Shannon information}.
]

The sharper formulation is:

[
\boxed{
\text{A distinction makes information possible;}
}
]

not:

[
\boxed{
\text{every ontological difference literally is Shannon information.}
}
]

