# 3. “Physical existence requires at least two states”

This contains an important operational insight but overstates it ontologically.

## Valid operational claim

For something to be empirically detected, its presence must make some difference to an observation:

[
\text{presence}
\neq
\text{absence}.
]

Thus measurement requires at least two distinguishable possibilities in the **combined measurement situation**:

[
{\text{effect present},\text{effect absent}}.
]

## Invalid stronger claim

It does not follow that each existing thing must possess two internal states.

A stable entity may remain in one internal state while continuously producing a detectable effect. The relevant contrast can be:

[
\text{entity present}
\quad\text{versus}\quad
\text{entity absent},
]

not two states internal to the entity.

More importantly:

[
\text{measurability}
\neq
\text{existence}.
]

A scientific theory may infer entities indirectly or treat some structures as real despite their not being individually measurable in isolation.

So:

[
\boxed{
\text{empirical detectability requires distinguishable consequences}.
}
]

But:

[
\boxed{
\text{physical existence itself requires two internal states}
}
]

has not been demonstrated.

