# 7. Measurement problem, hard problem and incompleteness

They share an abstract pattern:

[
\text{a framework has difficulty accounting for a condition}
]

[
\text{that cannot be represented straightforwardly within its ordinary terms}.
]

But they are not the same technical problem.

## Gödel incompleteness

A proof-theoretic result concerning formal arithmetic systems.

## Quantum measurement problem

A problem concerning the relation among unitary evolution, measurement outcomes, probabilities and definite events.

## Hard problem

A philosophical problem concerning why physical or functional organization is accompanied by phenomenal character.

Rovelli’s relational quantum mechanics does not claim that conscious observers collapse reality. It treats every physical system as potentially playing the observer role and regards measurements as ordinary interactions producing relational facts. ([aXi][3])

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{quantum observer}
\neq
\text{phenomenal subject}.
}
]

The measurement problem cannot be used without further argument to establish consciousness as constitutive of physical actuality.

The most that can be claimed is:

[
\boxed{
\text{all three involve limits on a naive view-from-nowhere.}
}
]

That is a philosophical parallel, not an identity theorem.

