# 8. “Science bypasses the observer”

This is partly insightful but too strong.

Science typically seeks:

* public methods;
* replicable observations;
* intersubjective agreement;
* observer-invariant regularities.

It does not literally remove all observers. It constrains observer-dependent variation through controlled procedures.

Nor is consciousness excluded by definition. Consciousness research uses:

* reports;
* behavioral discriminations;
* neural recordings;
* perturbations;
* no-report paradigms;
* first-person phenomenology.

The 2025 adversarial study of IIT and GNWT directly examined competing predictions about neural correlates of consciousness. Its mixed results challenged important predictions without eliminating consciousness from scientific investigation. ([Nature][4])

A stronger statement is:

[
\boxed{
\text{Third-person science describes publicly accessible structure}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{and therefore does not automatically capture first-person}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{manifestness merely by increasing structural detail.}
}
]

That is defensible.

But:

[
\boxed{
\text{science cannot find consciousness because it excludes}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{consciousness by construction}
}
]

is not.

