# 11. Markov blankets

The claim:

> a four-state Markov partition is necessary for any bounded system

is too strong.

A Markov blanket is a conditional-independence structure used to separate internal and external variables through boundary variables. Whether such blankets correspond to objective organismic boundaries, modeller-relative descriptions or only restricted classes of systems remains debated. Technical work has shown that sparse nonequilibrium systems do not automatically possess the required blanket structure, and some free-energy derivations need additional assumptions. ([PubMed Central (PMC)][5])

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{Markov blanket}
\neq
\text{necessary definition of every bounded physical system}.
}
]

And:

[
\boxed{
\text{Markov blanket}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{subject}.
}
]

It remains a useful model of conditional separation.

